Upgrading Nette
***************

.[perex]
`Form::FILLED` is now `Form::Filled`, `IRouter` is `Nette\Routing\Router`, and `invalidateControl()` is called `redrawControl()`. Nette libraries have made over 900 such changes since version 3.0, and the package `dresscode/upgrade-nette` knows every one of them: DressCode fixes roughly 700 right away, and for the rest it says what to write instead.

Two moves have a page of their own: from Nette 2.4 to [Nette 3 |upgrade-to-nette-3], and to [Nette 4 |upgrade-to-nette-4], that is, to `nette/utils`, `nette/php-generator`, `nette/mail` and `nette/robot-loader` in version 4.


Installation
============

```shell
composer require --dev dresscode/upgrade-nette phpstan/phpstan
vendor/bin/dresscode upgrade today
```

These two lines are enough: `upgrade today` finds the data and the rules of the package on its own, needs no configuration, and leaves the style of the code as it is. [Upgrading Code |upgrade] describes how to go one major version at a time and what to do with what is left. The data of each library applies only when the project uses that library, and only for the versions the project has reached. Installed into the project like this, DressCode needs the project to run on PHP 8.4 to 8.6; see [Installation |getting-started#Installation].

If you want every check to catch the old API too, add three things to the configuration: the types, the set `deprecations`, and the name of the package, which enables its rules in the section `nette`, as described in [How Upgrading Libraries Works |upgrading-libraries#Enabling It in the Configuration]:

```neon
typeAnalysis: phpstan

use:
	- nette           # your standard
	- deprecations
	- upgrade-nette
```

Together with the data, the set `deprecations` turns on the map `nette.flagsAsNamedArguments`, which converts flags to named arguments. The data also turns on two decisions, one for link destinations and one for component hooks: `nette.linkArguments` from `nette/application` 3.3 and `nette.monitorCallbacks` from `nette/component-model` 3.0. You do not have to add them to the configuration, and `upgrade today` runs them too. If you do not want one of them, turn it off in the section `nette` with the value `keep`.


What Gets Fixed
===============

This is a model class written for Nette 3.0, in a project that now uses the current versions:

```php .[before]
public function add(string $code): void
{
	$section = $this->session->getSection('cart');
	$items = $section->items ?? [];  // Method `Nette\Http\SessionSection::__get()` is replaced by `get($name)`.
	$items[] = Strings::match($code, '~^[A-Z]+-\d+$~', PREG_OFFSET_CAPTURE);  // Static method `Nette\Utils\Strings::match()` takes `captureOffset: true` instead of `PREG_OFFSET_CAPTURE`.
	$section->items = $items;  // Method `Nette\Http\SessionSection::__set()` is replaced by `set($name, $value)`.
	$section->source = $this->request->getQuery('utm_source', 'direct');  // Method `Nette\Http\SessionSection::__set()` is replaced by `set($name, $value)`.
	                                                                      // Method `Nette\Http\IRequest::getQuery()` is replaced by `getQuery($key) ?? $default`.
}
```

After `dresscode upgrade today`:

```php .[after]
public function add(string $code): void
{
	$section = $this->session->getSection('cart');
	$items = $section->items ?? [];
	$items[] = Strings::match($code, '~^[A-Z]+-\d+$~', captureOffset: true);
	$section->set('items', $items);
	$section->set('source', $this->request->getQuery('utm_source') ?? 'direct');
}
```

The line `$items = $section->items ?? []` was left alone: on the left of `??`, PHP first asks `SessionSection` whether the property is set, while `get()` does not ask. So the fix is [risky |configuration#risky-fixes], and DressCode makes it once you accept it in `dresscode upgrade today --ask-risky`, or once you list the decision `upgrading.api.replacedCalls` in the key `fixRisky`.

What the package fixes:

- **renamed classes and interfaces**: `Nette\Configurator` to `Nette\Bootstrap\Configurator`, `IRouter` to `Nette\Routing\Router`, `IControl` to `Nette\Forms\Control` and others; the import, the type, `new`, `instanceof` and the attribute are all rewritten,
- **constants** that used to be in uppercase: `Form::FILLED` to `Form::Filled`, `IResponse::S404_NOT_FOUND` to `IResponse::S404_NotFound`, `Presenter::INVALID_LINK_WARNING` to `InvalidLinkWarning`, including renames chained across several versions,
- **renamed methods**: `invalidateControl()` to `redrawControl()`, `getUnsafeValues()` to `getUntrustedValues()`, `Strings::contains()` to `str_contains()`,
- **renamed parameters** in calls with named arguments: `setExpiration(time: ...)` to `setExpiration(expire: ...)`, `Arrays::map(callback: ...)` to `Arrays::map(transformer: ...)`, also in `parent::__construct()` of a child class,
- **the default value after `??`**: `getParameter('page', 1)` to `getParameter('page') ?? 1`, and the same for `getQuery()`, `getPost()`, `getCookie()`, `getHeader()` and `getOption()`,
- **magic properties** to getters and setters: `$form->action`, `$control->caption`, `$section->user`, `$section['user']`,
- **flags** to named arguments: `Json::encode($value, Json::PRETTY)` and `Json::decode()`, `Strings::match()`, `matchAll()`, `split()` and `Arrays::grep()` with the constants `PREG_*`, `Neon::encode()` with `Neon::BLOCK`, the router's `add()`, `prepend()` and `addRoute()` with `Router::ONE_WAY`, `User::setExpiration()` with `IUserStorage::CLEAR_IDENTITY`, and the Tester's `Job::run()` with `RUN_ASYNC`,
- **calls of a different shape**: `addUpload($name, $label, true)` to `addMultiUpload()`, `getValues(true)` to `getValues('array')`, `Callback::closure()` to `Closure::fromCallable()`,
- **annotations** to attributes: `@persistent` and `@crossOrigin` in presenters, `@filter` and `@function` in Latte template parameter classes, and the attribute `#[CrossOrigin]` to `#[Requires(sameOrigin: false)]`,
- **the component hooks** `attached()` and `detached()` to callbacks of `monitor()` in the constructor, and **the query string and fragment in a link destination**, `link('Product:show?id=1#reviews')`, to arguments.

The map `nette.flagsAsNamedArguments` rewrites the flags according to the data; the decisions `nette.monitorCallbacks` and `nette.linkArguments` rewrite the hooks and the link destinations. Their rules come with the package. The rules of the DressCode engine do the rest, following the data.


What Is Left to You
===================

Where there is no replacement, or the replacement is used differently, DressCode reports the place and says what to write. This covers, for example, methods of `Url` and `UrlScript` that moved elsewhere, constants that disappeared without a replacement, or an `attached()` hook where DressCode cannot tell which type it was called for:

```
Method `Nette\Http\UrlScript::setPath()` is forbidden: the class is immutable; take a changed copy with `withPath()`.
Constant `Nette\Application\IRouter::SECURED` is forbidden: write the protocol, `https://`, into the mask of the route.
```

What a library only quietly marks as deprecated is not in the data. With the types, DressCode reports it anyway, and where the annotation names the replacement, it rewrites the code through `upgrading.declarations.deprecatedMember`. The data does not capture changes in behavior, in NEON configuration or in the syntax of Latte templates at all; you will find those in the upgrade guides of the individual libraries.


What the Package Covers
=======================

| library | versions |
|---|---|
| `nette/application` | 3.0 to 3.3 |
| `nette/assets` | 1.0 |
| `nette/bootstrap` | 3.0 to 3.2 |
| `nette/caching` | 3.0 to 3.4 |
| `nette/command-line` | 1.9 to 2.0 |
| `nette/component-model` | 3.0 to 4.0 |
| `nette/database` | 3.0 to 3.2 |
| `nette/di` | 3.0 to 3.2 |
| `nette/forms` | 3.0 to 3.3 |
| `nette/http` | 3.0 to 3.4 |
| `nette/mail` | 3.1 to 4.1 |
| `nette/neon` | 3.2 to 3.4 |
| `nette/php-generator` | 3.3 to 4.2 |
| `nette/robot-loader` | 3.2 to 4.1 |
| `nette/routing` | 3.0 to 3.1 |
| `nette/schema` | 1.2 to 1.3 |
| `nette/security` | 3.0 to 3.2 |
| `nette/tester` | 2.2 to 2.6 |
| `nette/utils` | 3.0 to 4.1 |
| `latte/latte` | 3.0 to 3.1 |
| `tracy/tracy` | 2.6 to 2.10 |

The versions show the releases in which a library changed something the data captures; a newer version that renamed nothing needs no new data. An application on Nette 2.4 is upgraded the same way, with version 3.0 as the first step; [Upgrading Code |upgrade#One Major Version at a Time, by Hand] describes how to proceed one major version at a time.

